==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|/dʒaɪb/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪb|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
Probably from {{der|en|nl|gijben}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}, {{m|nl|gijpen}}; cognate with {{cog|da|gibbe}}, {{cog|de|gieben}}, {{m|de|giepen}}, {{cog|sv|gipa}}, {{m|sv|gippa}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|part of speech=v|id=82805|date=1900}}</ref>

The noun is derived from the verb; compare {{der|en|nl|gjib}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}, {{m|nl|gjip||act of gybing; a boom}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|part of speech=''n.{{sup|2}}''|noformat=yes|id=82804|date=1900}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb|gyb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical}} To [[shift#Verb|shift]] a [[fore-and-aft]] [[sail#Noun|sail]] [[forcefully]] and [[suddenly]] from one side of a [[sailing vessel]] to the other, while [[sail#Verb|sailing]] before the [[wind#Noun|wind]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical}} Of a fore-and-aft sail or its [[boom#Noun|boom]]: to shift forcefully and suddenly from one side of a sailing vessel to the other.
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|page=271|passage={{...}} I had my Man ''Friday'' to teach as to what belong'd to the Navigation of my Boat; for though he knew very well how to paddle a Canoe, he knew nothing what belong'd to a Sail and a Rudder, and was the moſt amaz'd when he ſaw me work the Boat too and again in the Sea by the Rudder, and how the Sail '''gyb'd''', and fill'd this Way or that Way, as the Courſe we ſail'd chang'd: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical}} Generally of a small sailing vessel: to [[change tack]] with the wind [[cross#Verb|crossing]] behind the vessel.
# {{lb|en|by extension|obsolete}} ''Often as'' '''gybe at''': to [[balk]], [[hesitate]], or [[vacillate]] when [[face#Verb|faced]] with a [[course of action]], [[plan#Noun|plan]], or [[proposal]].

=====Usage notes=====
Sense 3 (“to change tack”) is generally used of [[boat]]s and other small sailing craft; the corresponding manoeuvre in a [[sailing ship]] is {{m|en|wear}}.

